---
layout: layouts/page.njk
permalink: /
bodyClass: page page--home
---
<div class="home" data-pagefind-body>
  <header class="home__hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">{{ site.title }}</p>
    <h1 class="home__title">{{ site.tagline }}</h1>
    <p class="home__lede">{{ site.description }}</p>
  </header>

  {#
    Search, before any list of games.

    The home page is where somebody arrives without a game in mind, and the
    header's trigger is a small control in the corner among three others —
    fine as a tool while reading, too quiet to be the way in. So this is the
    same search, said at the size of the job: one field-shaped control across
    the column, above everything it searches.

    It is not a second search box. There is one search surface on this site and
    this opens it, which is why it is a button and not an input — an input here
    would have to grow its own results list, its own keyboard handling and its
    own idea of scope, and then be kept in step with the palette's. Typing on
    it still works: the first keystroke opens the panel and lands in the field.

    Hidden until the script that opens it runs, like the header's trigger.
  #}
  <button
    type="button"
    class="home-search"
    data-palette-open
    aria-haspopup="dialog"
    aria-label="Search all games"
    hidden>
    <svg class="home-search__icon" viewBox="0 0 20 20" width="20" height="20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <circle cx="9" cy="9" r="5.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/>
      <path d="M13.2 13.2 17 17" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/>
    </svg>
    <span class="home-search__label">Search every game, section and term</span>
    <kbd class="home-search__key" aria-hidden="true">⌘K</kbd>
  </button>

  {#
    Favorites, then recent. Both are shells the client fills, because only the
    browser knows what is in them — and both stay hidden until there is
    something to put in them, so a first-time reader meets the games and
    nothing else.

    Favorites sits above because it is the deliberate list: a reader who has
    starred a game has said it matters more than the fact that they opened
    something yesterday. Recent drops anything already starred, so the two rows
    never show the same game twice.
  #}
  <section class="home__section" aria-labelledby="favorites-title" data-favorite-games hidden>
    <h2 class="section-title" id="favorites-title">Favorite Games</h2>
    <ul class="game-grid game-grid--recent" data-favorite-list></ul>
  </section>

  {#
    Recently opened. Empty and hidden until the history says otherwise, which
    it can only do from the browser — so this stays a shell the client fills.
  #}
  <section class="home__section" aria-labelledby="recent-title" data-recent-games hidden>
    <h2 class="section-title" id="recent-title">Recent Games</h2>
    <ul class="game-grid game-grid--recent" data-recent-list></ul>
  </section>

  <section class="home__section" aria-labelledby="games-title">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="games-title">
      All Games
      <span class="section-title__count">{{ listed | length }}</span>
    </h2>

    <ul class="game-grid">
      {% for slug, item in listed %}
        <li class="game-tile" data-game-slug="{{ slug }}">
          {#
            The box art is shown as itself: contained in a fixed-ratio frame on
            a quiet ground, so covers of different proportions still line up
            across the grid without being cropped. Nothing is laid over the
            art — the title sits below it, where it can be set in the same type
            as the rest of the site.
          #}
          <a class="game-tile__link" href="{{ item.url }}">
            {% if item.meta.box_art %}
              <span class="game-tile__art">
                <img class="game-tile__image" src="{{ item.meta.box_art }}" alt="" sizes="320px">
              </span>
            {% endif %}
          </a>

          <div class="game-tile__body">
            <h3 class="game-tile__title"><a href="{{ item.url }}">{{ item.title }}</a></h3>
            <p class="game-tile__meta">
              {{ item.meta.designer }}{% if item.meta.year_published %} · {{ item.meta.year_published }}{% endif %}
            </p>
            <p class="game-tile__description">{{ item.meta.description }}</p>
            <ul class="chip-row">
              {% for type in item.contentTypes %}
                <li><a class="chip" href="{{ type.url }}">{{ type.label }}</a></li>
              {% endfor %}
              {% for expansion in item.expansions %}
                <li><a class="chip chip--muted" href="{{ expansion.contentTypes[0].url }}">{{ expansion.title }}</a></li>
              {% endfor %}
            </ul>
          </div>
        </li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>
